DEFECTS - tb/base.7z
====================

Defects in the vendored 2001 material inside tb/base.7z that are known and
deliberately NOT repaired. The archive is an input, not source this project
maintains: its sha256 is recorded in tb/base2001.PROVENANCE and the ten
projekt/<p>/www8051.rom files it ships are pinned in tb/reference.md5, so a
repack moves the reference every gate run is judged against.

Archive measured for this file: sha256
e4cdbb943b96f589e2f072e9355e261e281c6ddcd84e529a1422d585e33163bb, 132549
bytes, which is the hash tb/base2001.PROVENANCE already records.

Why a note and not a patch. tb/isa/testall-fixes.patch is the other half of
this rule: there a vendored third-party file has a defect and the defect IS
repaired, because tb/sim/run-testall.sh copies testall.asm to a work
directory and assembles it on every `make sim' run, so the correction has a
run to attach to and the vendored bytes still never change. Nothing in the
gate assembles the file below. A .patch beside it would be a repair that
never runs, and running it is precisely what must not happen: see WHY IT IS
LEFT.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. lib/http.asm line 98 - `cjne a,0xFF' should be `cjne a,#0xFF'
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Lines 95-100 of lib/http.asm, the 16-bit decrement of data_len:

    ;; data_len--;
        dec     data_len+1      ;LSB
        mov     a,data_len+1
        cjne    a,0xFF,nodecdlen        <-- line 98
        dec     data_len        ;MSB
    nodecdlen:

The borrow into the high byte is meant to be taken when the low byte just
wrapped 0x00 -> 0xFF, so the operand is meant to be the constant 255. Written
without `#' it is a direct address instead. The instruction assembles as
CJNE A,direct,rel, opcode 0xB5, and compares A against the byte read from
direct address 0xFF - which is in SFR space and unimplemented on the 8051,
so the value is whatever the part returns for an absent SFR. The borrow is
then taken, or skipped, for a reason unconnected to the counter.

What it should be: `cjne a,#0xFF,nodecdlen', which is CJNE A,#data,rel,
opcode 0xB4. Same length, one opcode byte different.

The `#' is not optional style. Every other comparison against a literal in
the file carries it (`cjne a,#CR', `cjne a,#'g'', `cjne a,#8'), and the places
that do mean a direct operand name a register-bank address rather than a
number (`cjne a,ar2,ROMlop'), so line 98 is the only comparison in the file
whose operand form disagrees with its comment.


WHY IT IS LEFT
--------------

Because the defect is not a transcription slip in the .asm. It is in the
2001 binary as well, and the binary is what the testbench links.

  - No gate stage assembles lib/*.asm. tb/Makefile's check and oracle targets
    run `make' only inside projekt/<p>, and each of those Makefiles takes the
    library as a prebuilt file: nine link $(LIBDIR)/libw80.a via `-lw80',
    serial links $(LIBDIR)/libw23.a via `-lw23'. lib/Makefile does carry an
    http.obj rule and a libw.a rule, but nothing in tb/ ever descends into
    lib/ to run them.

  - The prebuilt member carries the same encoding. In base.7z's
    lib/libw80.a, member http.obj, at .text offset 0x4c:

        70 02  05 00  15 00  e5 00  b5 ff 02  15 00  e5 00  25

    which is jnz +2 / inc data_addr / dec data_len+1 / mov a,data_len+1 /
    CJNE A,0FFh,+2 / dec data_len / mov a,state80, and then the 0x25 opcode
    of `add a,acc'. The direct operands are still 0x00, awaiting their
    relocations; the 0xFF is not, because it is written as a constant.
    Opcode 0xB5, the direct form. Hand-counting the instruction lengths from
    the top of .text in lib/http.asm reaches 0x4c at that same line, so the
    object is the source, assembled as written.

    The identical bytes sit at the identical offset in base2001.7z's
    lib/libw80.a, which section 1 of tb/base2001.PROVENANCE shows is copied
    byte-for-byte out of the 2001 gnu13 tarball, and in base.7z's own
    lib/lib23_old/libw23.a. Three copies of the 2001 build, one encoding.

  - Nine of the ten pinned reference ROMs already contain the instruction,
    at these offsets in projekt/<p>/www8051.rom as base.7z ships it:

        ds1620   0x0fd5      led2     0x0bae
        ds1822   0x0f55      led3     0x0c6c
        lcd      0x0de4      serial   0x1785
        led1     0x0c51      welcome  0x0ae8
                             wjava    0x0ae8

    diag is the exception, and only because it links no CGI objects and no
    web51_80.obj, so nothing references a symbol http.obj defines and the
    archive member is never pulled in; its 1267-byte ROM holds no copy.

So repairing lib/http.asm inside the archive would leave the source and the
shipped library disagreeing, and rebuilding the library from the repaired
source would change one byte in nine ROMs: every md5 in tb/reference.md5
except diag's, and the `make oracle' comparison against the untouched
projekt/*/www8051.hex, which is the one record of what the 2001 port emitted
and cannot be regenerated.

That is a deliberate change to the reference, not hygiene. If it is ever
wanted it belongs in a commit of its own that repacks base.7z, re-pins
tb/reference.md5 and teaches tb/hexoracle.py to expect the delta, with this
note updated to say so. Until then the archive stays as it is and the defect
is recorded here so it cannot be lost.
